"A call graph connects functions or methods according to possible or observed calls.\n\nUse when: Trace a feature’s execution dependencies across functions. Locate recursion or inspect the impact of changing a callee.\n\nAvoid when: Exact call order or control branches within one function are needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes represent functions or methods with concise qualified names. Caller-to-callee arrows encode invocation possibility or observation. A legend distinguishes direct, indirect, inferred, and observed calls.\n\nBuild: Choose static analysis or a specific runtime capture and state its limitations. Select entry points and a bounded exploration depth. Represent unresolved dynamic dispatch explicitly rather than guessing targets. Place common callees on shared ranks and route recursion around the outside.\n\nCheck: The graph does not imply every possible call occurred. Indirect call uncertainty remains visible. Observed counts, if included, state their source and capture window. Recursion arcs and labels avoid node interiors.\n\nPitfalls: Interpreting spatial order as an execution sequence. Presenting a partial dynamic trace as a complete program call graph.\n\nExample: Checkout.submit calls Price.quote and Payment.authorize, while a retry helper forms a documented recursive path.\n\nAlternatives: Control-flow graph, Sequence diagram."
